  "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+1?$",
  "worked_solution": "We start with plotting the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/ca12bd6db97d982fd2a9e73dcd9d5da0248ce1a40e582787c3f73480f338a570.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $\\frac{3}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/53d2977f07e132aa0e3c3608e8fef82f2d058fb1a99aa828ae8b317b8de6844a.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+1.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $1$ unit up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ to $(0,\\frac{5}{2})$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931416/assets/6adc189f0d16cf60044553c13d79297c04f4fbfb2806a9bde6971b203822f17a.png)",
